17592186575498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186574958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575498 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575498 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575498 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575498 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186575243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575498 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c86deb60-271b-56a0-b579-07d9a6af8966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575518 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186575362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575518 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575518 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575518 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186575043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575518 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ea8e249e-3dee-5215-9e54-0f6bd4c6007a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575526 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575526 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575526 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $(8a+2b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $b\\cdot a=4(a\\cdot a)$\n\nII. $-a\\cdot a<0$\n\nIII. $b\\cdot a=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02eb1a38-d05c-54e9-9813-73779e1c0487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575526 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Simplifying the expression $(8a+2b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8a+2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ (8a)\\cdot a+(2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 8(a\\cdot a)+2(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 2(b\\cdot a) & =-8(a\\cdot a) \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-4(a\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $a\\ne 0,$ we must have $a\\cdot a>0$, which implies $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false since it leads to a contradiction. If $b\\cdot a=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8a+2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 8(a\\cdot a)+2(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 8(a\\cdot a)+2(4) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut this is a contradiction since $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575548 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${b}_{2}=k{b}_{1}-a$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2}=0.$ If $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, find the value of $k.$ \n\n*Hint: You might wish to take the equation ${b}_{2}=k{b}_{1}-a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a4c9f12-f1ec-53fb-81d9-ded6a93d72ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575548 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the equation ${b}_{2}=k{b}_{1}-a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & =k{b}_{1}-a \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (k{b}_{1}-a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (k{b}_{1})-({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =k({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})-({b}_{1}\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the formula for the dot product to compute the values of ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1} & ={b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot a & ={b}_{1}{a}_{1}+{b}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1},$ ${b}_{1}\\cdot a,$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =k({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})-({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ 0 & =k\\cdot 4-2 \\\\ -4k & =-2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575550 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575550 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575550 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575550 #edb/ref 1071 "If $u=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ and $u\\cdot v=-2,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (10v)}{u\\cdot (4u)}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575550 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61c2f333-f089-53c4-9125-104e2258d303" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575550 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $u\\cdot (10v),$ we multiply $u\\cdot v$ by $10.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (10v) & =10(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (4u),$ we compute $u\\cdot u$ first and then multiply the result by $4.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (4u) & =4(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =4({u}_{1}^{2}+{u}_{2}^{2}+{u}_{3}^{2}+{u}_{4}^{2}) \\\\  & =4((-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(1+4+1+4+1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{u\\cdot (10v)}{u\\cdot (4u)}=\\frac{-20}{44}=-\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575554 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575554 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575554 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575554 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\alpha \\beta =12$ and $a\\cdot b=4$, then what is $(\\alpha b)\\cdot (\\beta a)-21?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575554 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64386ab8-01dd-5ca3-af09-05de11ca4efa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575554 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in both variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha b)\\cdot (\\beta a)-21 & =\\alpha (b\\cdot \\beta a)-21 \\\\  & =\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using that the dot product is symmetric, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)-21 & =\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\alpha \\beta$ and $a\\cdot b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)-21 & =12\\cdot (4)-21 \\\\  & =48-21 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575577 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575577 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575577 #edb/ref 1071 "If $u=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,,$ and $u\\cdot v=22,$ then $\\frac{(3u)\\cdot v}{u\\cdot (2u)}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575577 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "955d6eb2-9fbf-5873-9d9e-5edc1cabeb28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575577 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $3u\\cdot v,$ we multiply $u\\cdot v$ by $3.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3u)\\cdot v & =3(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (2u),$ we compute $u\\cdot u$ first and then multiply the result by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (2u) & =2(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =2({u}_{1}^{2}+{u}_{2}^{2}+{u}_{3}^{2}+{u}_{4}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(4+1+9+25) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 39 \\\\  & =78.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(3u)\\cdot v}{u\\cdot (2u)}=\\frac{66}{78}=\\frac{11}{13}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575604 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575604 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575604 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575604 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $b\\cdot a<0$\n\nII. $b\\cdot a=3$\n\nIII. $a\\cdot a=-3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575604 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0f13f7c-58e9-5582-87a9-733c8480e4b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575604 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $a\\ne 0,$ we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575617 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186575449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575617 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575617 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575617 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${b}_{2}=p{b}_{1}+a$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2}=0.$ If $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$, find the value of $p.$ \n\n*Hint: You might wish to take the equation ${b}_{2}=p{b}_{1}+a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575617 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ebc01d60-f520-5118-a5cd-4a61bccfbcb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186575617 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we take the equation ${b}_{2}=p{b}_{1}+a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & =p{b}_{1}+a \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (p{b}_{1}+a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (p{b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =p({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the formula for the dot product to compute the values of ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1} & ={b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+(-3)^{2} \\\\  & =4+9 \\\\  & =13 \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot a & ={b}_{1}{a}_{1}+{b}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4+3 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1},$ ${b}_{1}\\cdot a,$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =p({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ 0 & =p\\cdot 13+7 \\\\ -13p & =7 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
